That command would be very convenient in some circumstances, i.e.
when you're lined up with the monster; but what about when you're not?
The ability to identify any monster you can see, not just the 8 directions,
is too important to sacrifice in this way.

Here's my idea for a solution:

Let '/' be strictly for identifying symbols.
'/'
	"Symbol to identify: "
'o'
	o -- orc (use 'f' for exact identification)

Let 'f' ("focus"?) be the command that identifies visible monsters.
(Or maybe 'X' "examine", now that X is no longer for Xscribing spellbooks.)
I like the idea that someone mentioned recently, about identifying all
monsters you pass over.  It might be easier, though, to do it like this:

You hit 'f'.
	"(directions, '.', '?' for help)" is displayed on message line.
You steer the cursor over to a monster.
You press '.'
	"o -- an orc (peaceful goblin)--More--"
You press spacebar, and the message line goes back to
	"(directions, '.', '?' for help)".
The cursor is still over the orc, and now you steer it from there
to some other spot and hit '.'.  Eventually you press ESC.


The advantages of this method (over keeping a list of monsters and identifying
them when the player is done steering the cursor) are:
1--Easier programming, no lists to keep.  No multiple identifies of same
   monster to eliminate.
2--Easier to see which monster is which.  If you get a list of identifies you
   will be saying, "Now, which 'i' did I pass over first?"  With the above
   method, you know which monster you were on when you hit '.' -- and
   even if you forgot, hit spacebar and the cursor goes back to it.
3--If there are a lot of monsters in the room, you don't have to identify ones
   that you must pass the cursor over.


One more suggestion that applies to steering the cursor over the screen,
in teleportation as well as in identifying things:
How about letting the CTRL-directions move the cursor a big step,
something like 8 spaces?  On slow dialups it can be a pain to hang on
the 'h' key for a while.


